Mount a volume group's volumes without asking for a password.

A volume group is served as one NFS export per volume: the group's own directory
first, then each volume mounted inside it. The first of those is made by whoever
is running the engine. The rest were escalated with sudo, always, and not
because anything needed it -- `elevate` is decided by how the process was
started, never by whether the mount would have worked without it.

macOS lets the owner of a directory mount an NFS export on it, which is exactly
how the parent mount was made a moment earlier. Mounting the children the same
way works: tested by hand against a running export, `mount -t nfs` on a
subdirectory of a share already mounted returns 0 as an ordinary user.

What the escalation did instead was ask for an administrator password in the
middle of opening a disk image -- and where nobody could type one, which is
every unattended case, print "need to use sudo to mount additional NFS exports"
and leave the group mounted read-only with an empty folder where each volume
should be. A person opening a Linux backup saw root, home and backup, all empty,
and nothing anywhere saying why.

Linux is left as it was: mounting there does need privilege, and the engine is
run differently.

--- a/anylinuxfs/src/cmd_mount.rs	2026-08-25 10:52:01
+++ b/anylinuxfs/src/cmd_mount.rs
@@ -1141,6 +1141,13 @@
             .peekable();
 
         let _log_guard = ConsoleLogGuard::enable_temporarily(verbose);
+        // The parent export was mounted by whoever is running this, and macOS
+        // lets them mount on a directory they own, so the volumes inside it
+        // need nothing extra. Asking for a password here asks for one to
+        // finish something that has already worked without it.
+        #[cfg(target_os = "macos")]
+        let elevate = false;
+        #[cfg(not(target_os = "macos"))]
         let elevate = self.config.common.privilege.sudo_uid.is_none()
             && self.config.common.privilege.invoker_uid != 0;
 
